  "customfield_10187": null,
  "description": {
   "content": [
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Given a Type 1 UUID pid, e.g. demo:8e25cab0-167d-11e1-be50-0800200c9a66, I would like to have the object stored on the filesystem based on the uuid-encoded timestamp, e.g.",
       "type": "text"
      },
      {
       "type": "hardBreak"
      },
      {
       "text": "$FEDORA_HOME/data/objectStore/2011/11/24/info%3Afedora%2Fdemo%3A8e25cab0-167d-11e1-be50-0800200c9a66.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "The timestamp-based path mapping should be configurable, e.g. yyyy/MM/dd, and yyyy/MM/dd is a reasonable default. The Path ID Mapper must be able to accommodate non-type1 UUID identifiers as well, falling back to the default, hash-based algorithm.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
     "content": [
      {
       "text": "Rationale: We mint UUIDs for >95% of our repository objects. It's also the case that it's only the most recently ingested/modified objects that are most frequently accessed. Given the scale of our repository, we'd like to be able to make storage policy decisions (slow/fast storage, backup policies) based on the age (i.e. importance) of the object. This also allows us to prioritize the restore of objects in a disaster recovery scenario.",
       "type": "text"
      }
     ],
     "type": "paragraph"
    }
   ],
